Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the application. Let me analyze the domains based on what I've discovered.

Based on my thorough investigation of the codebase, I can now identify the core business domains. Here's my analysis:

**Application Overview**: This is a Parts Unlimited ordering service (a refrigeration/freezer room equipment ordering system) with the following main functional areas:
1. **Product Catalog Management** - Managing catalog items (SKU, description, price, inventory, lead time)
2. **Dealer Management** - Managing dealer/supplier contact and location records
3. **Quote Management** - Creating and managing customer price quotes with line items
4. **Order Management** - Managing orders created from approved quotes with lifecycle events
5. **Shipment Management** - Managing shipment records and delivery tracking for orders

The database schema confirms: CatalogItems, Dealers, Quotes (with QuoteAdditionalItemRecord), Orders (with OrderEvents), and ShipmentRecords (with ShipmentEventInfo, DeliveryAddress, PhoneInfo).

The controllers map clearly to these domains: `/catalog`, `/dealers`, `/quotes`, `/orders`, `/shipments`.

```json
{
  "business_domains": [
    {
      "domain_name": "Product Catalog",
      "description": "This domain manages the product catalog, which is the central repository of all items available for purchase in the ordering system. It encompasses the lifecycle management of catalog items including their creation, retrieval, update, and removal. Each catalog item is uniquely identified by a SKU number and carries product attributes such as description, price, available inventory count, and lead time. This domain provides the foundational reference data that other domains (particularly quoting) rely upon when specifying which products are included in a quote or order. The bounded context covers all business logic related to maintaining an accurate and up-to-date product catalog, as well as the HTTP interface for catalog item operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE on /catalog endpoint) and the underlying data persistence layer for catalog data."
    },
    {
      "domain_name": "Dealer Management",
      "description": "This domain manages the network of dealers and suppliers that participate in the ordering process. A dealer is a business entity with identifying information including name, contact person, address, email, and phone number. Dealers are associated with quotes, as each quote is issued by a specific dealer on behalf of a customer. This domain is responsible for maintaining accurate dealer records throughout their lifecycle: registration, information update, and removal from the system. It provides the dealer reference data that the quoting process depends on for validation and association. The bounded context covers all business logic and data persistence for dealer entities, as well as the HTTP interface for dealer CRUD operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE on /dealers endpoint)."
    },
    {
      "domain_name": "Quote Management",
      "description": "This domain handles the sales quoting process, which is the mechanism by which potential orders are proposed and negotiated. A quote represents a formal price proposal made to a customer by a dealer, containing one or more line items referencing catalog products (by SKU and quantity), pricing information (total cost and discount), customer and dealer identification, delivery location details (city, postal code, state), and an expiration date. Quotes serve as the precursor to orders — an order is created from an approved quote. This domain manages the full lifecycle of quotes: creation, retrieval (by ID or customer name), update, and deletion. It also validates that associated dealers exist when creating or updating a quote. The bounded context covers all business logic surrounding quotation, including customer-dealer negotiation data, quote line items, pricing calculations, and the HTTP interface for quote operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE on /quotes endpoint)."
    },
    {
      "domain_name": "Order Management",
      "description": "This domain governs the order lifecycle after a quote has been accepted and converted into a confirmed order. An order is created from an existing quote and tracks the progression of a customer's purchase from creation through fulfillment. Each order has a unique order ID, references its originating quote, records the order date, maintains a current status (e.g., Created, Confirmed, Fulfilled), and maintains a chronological event log of activities and status changes. This domain is responsible for creating orders from quotes (ensuring the quote exists and hasn't already been used), retrieving orders (by ID, dealer name, or status), updating order details and status, appending order events, and deleting orders. The bounded context includes all business rules related to order processing, state transitions, event tracking, and order validation, as well as the HTTP interface for order operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE on /orders endpoint)."
    },
    {
      "domain_name": "Shipment and Delivery",
      "description": "This domain manages the physical fulfillment and logistics aspect of the ordering process, tracking how confirmed orders are physically delivered to customers. Each order corresponds to at most one shipment record, which captures the delivery date, delivery address, contact information (contact name, primary and alternate phone numbers), and a chronological log of shipment events (e.g., dispatched, in transit, delivered). The domain also supports the concept of a 'delivery' — an aggregate view that combines shipment information with its related order and quote details to provide a complete delivery picture. It handles the creation of shipment records for existing orders, updates to shipment details, tracking of shipment event milestones, retrieval of shipments by status (including filtering by DeliveryConfirmed status), and deletion of shipment records. The bounded context covers all business logic and data persistence for shipment tracking, delivery address management, phone contact information, and the HTTP interface for shipment operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE on /shipments endpoint)."
    }
  ]
}
```

